From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kandelo <noreply@kandelo.invalid>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Support the isolated Kandelo bottle publisher

Kandelo's bottle publisher gives Formula evaluation root-owned,
read-only Homebrew source and trust state. Homebrew normally requires
its repository to be writable and persists a redundant per-item trust
entry for every fully qualified install, even when the containing tap is
already trusted. Both behaviors reject the publisher's intentionally
immutable state before a Formula can run.

The publisher also preinstalls reviewed dependencies before asking
Homebrew to build the selected Formula with `--ignore-dependencies`.
That flag must prevent Homebrew from recursively installing native
packages into the target Cellar, but the build child currently
interprets it by dropping every dependency from Superenv. Direct build
tools such as WABT and Binaryen then disappear from the Formula PATH
even though their sealed proxy kegs are installed.

Homebrew on Linux also injects native sandbox dependencies such as
Bubblewrap into every Formula. Those host dependencies must remain
available to native Homebrew, but recording them in a Kandelo target
bottle would make guest Homebrew treat Linux executables as WebAssembly
runtime dependencies.

Exclude only the Homebrew repository from the preinstall writability
diagnostic and skip automatic per-item persistence when the tap already
grants the same authority. When the isolated publisher supplies its
protected static plan, populate the build child's environment with only
the matching direct native build dependencies. Its schema distinguishes
literal Formula dependencies from statically allowlisted Requirements.
Validate each evaluated Requirement's class, Formula identity, sentinel
executable, and tags against the sealed plan, then reconstruct only
those matching Formulae as build-only Superenv inputs.  Do not
recursively expand them. Every selected dependency must already be
present as a proxy keg, so a missing proxy still fails closed.  For a
Requirement also tagged `:test`, validate that its sealed proxy contains
the planned sentinel and expose only that proxy's standard tool and
metadata paths to Homebrew's Formula test process.  Use that same
protected plan to suppress Linux global dependencies for the identified
Kandelo target and every Formula in its exact immutable target taps
while Homebrew derives the recursive target receipt. Formulae from other
taps and the separate native Homebrew prefix retain normal Linux
dependency resolution.  While that plan is active, also prevent `brew
test` from automatically pouring Bubblewrap into the target Cellar. A
usable host Bubblewrap can still be used, but an unavailable sandbox
cannot bypass the publisher's reviewed native plan.  The publisher
retains an embedded receipt for Kandelo's static VFS composer, so make
same-environment retries insensitive to tap-only commits with Homebrew's
upstream GNU tar flags.  Remove only the tap Git head from a fresh copy
of the temporary archived receipt and restore the exact installed
receipt after bottling. Normalize the archive file time to the stable
source time used by Homebrew's tar and gzip paths.  Every other required
Homebrew path remains subject to the normal writability check, and
explicit `brew trust` operations still use the normal mutation path and
fail against an immutable publisher store.

The sealed tapped checkout is also workflow-owned, so the isolated Formula
identity cannot use Homebrew's ordinary Git query without Git rejecting the
foreign ownership. Resolve only the selected tap through the root-owned
publisher plan: require its canonical read-only checkout path, run the
launcher-validated Git with command-scoped `safe.directory` in an otherwise
empty environment, and require the observed commit to equal the plan before
the receipt or bottle uses it. Other taps retain Homebrew's ordinary lookup.

This patch is applied only to the publisher's temporary Homebrew
overlay. Guest Homebrew keeps its normal repository, trust, and
dependency behavior.
---
